  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 AM EDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis / Forecast:\n\nCorridor of quick flow aloft guided by two dominant features spanning\nthe CONUS... longwave trough over the Pacific NW and a stout thermal\nridge over the southern CONUS. Result is a ridge axis that noses its \nnorthern zenith into the Dakotas, with a corridor of WNW to NW flow stretching\nthrough the Corn Belt and into the lower Great Lakes. Stationary front \ncurrently analyzed from northeast Colorado through Kansas City, spanning \neast from there through Peoria, IL through Akron, OH. This front will act\nas the primary forcing mechanism at the surface as a series of deep Pacific\ntropical waves zip out of the Gulf of California and eject into the Plains. \nReturn flow will lead to a northward draw of this front into \nthe weekend, but stout surface high pressure over Ontario and \nresultant dry northeasterly low-level flow will provide a \nbarrier over northern Michigan in regard to this deeper moisture\nadvancing much, if any, into the region this weekend. The front\nwill be forced back south into the weekend, with dry Canadian \nsurface high pressure dominating to start next week. \n\nDetails:\n\nLikely dry today as moisture intrusion is held off by aforementioned dry \nnortheast flow from Canada. Clouds will increase aloft, but that is about\nthe only indication that a robust rainfall event is about to approach the \narea. Key word here being - approach. \n\nGuidance has continued a pessimistic trend regarding this intruding rain.\nConvection to really blossom across Minnesota and Iowa through the day today\ninto Saturday, with resultant rain plume riding WNW to NW flow regime along\nthe increasingly northward displaced frontal boundary. Latest guidance wants\nto keep most of, if not, all of the CWA dry through the weekend now... with\nperhaps some shower activity over Manistee and that is about it. Bigger story\nwill be the temperatures... being on the cold side of the front, temperatures\nwill be seasonably cool at best on the warmest days next week... with what\nlooks like our first true stretch of fall-like days on the docket. The chilliest\nof the days looks to be Monday as the core of a cooler airmass builds overhead,\nwhich may lead to a handful of spots failing to get much past 55 degrees for \ndaytime high temperatures. For reference, average highs in mid-late September\nare 66 to 71 degrees, The big thing to note... is that while we embark on this\ncooler stretch of weather, rain chances look to be rather minimal, so at this \njuncture, anticipation is that much of next week will hold on the dry side.",
